[about]
Title=05 - Tag Files and Copy Them
Intro=Tagging two files with the semicolon and copying both at once: the one idea behind every FileDir command that acts on several files. This walk assumes walk four.
Setup=A folder of files is open.
Homework=Tag three files and move them to a folder of your own.

[step]

Say=Arrow to a file you want, and press the semicolon to tag it. Tagging marks a file for the next command without moving you.
Key=Semicolon
Hear=Tagged
Note=The slash key untags. Shift plus Home tags everything from here to the top.

[step]

Say=Arrow to another file and tag that one too. FileDir says how many are tagged as the number changes.
Key=Semicolon
Hear=2 tagged files
Note=Alt plus Y says the count at any time.

[step]

Say=Press Control plus C to copy the tagged files. FileDir asks where to.
Key=Control+C
Hear=Copy To
Hear=Folder edit
Note=The window is named for the command. The box remembers the last ten answers.

[step]

Say=Type the folder, or press Alt plus B to browse for it, then Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=2 files copied
Note=Nothing is moved unless you use Control plus M instead, which moves.

[step]

Say=Other ways to tag. Spacebar toggles the current item; the greater-than key tags and moves to the next, so a run of files is tagged as fast as you can press it; the less-than key untags the same way. Control plus A tags all, Control plus Shift plus A clears all.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Among the tagged, Shift plus N and Shift plus P go to the next and previous tagged item; Shift plus B and Shift plus E to the first and last. The Apostrophe says whether the item you are on is tagged.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=What a tag feeds. Shift plus C copies the tagged files to a folder you choose; Shift plus M moves them; Shift plus Z zips them into one archive; Delete recycles them, after asking once. The same tags, a different verb.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus C, Control plus X and Control plus V copy, cut and paste tagged files through the clipboard, as in Windows Explorer -- so files from two folders can be gathered and pasted in a third. Alt plus C appends to the clipboard without clearing it.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A planned misstep. Moving to a folder that already holds a file of the same name: FileDir asks whether to overwrite, and says whether the one there is older or newer, so the answer is informed.
Key=
Hear=Overwrite report dot d o c x? The existing file is newer. Yes button

[step]

Say=Escape keeps both. Control plus S saves the tag states of this window; Control plus Shift plus S restores them -- for when a long selection must survive a detour.
Key=Escape
Hear=FileDir, List box

[step]

Say=Tagging by rule. Alt plus Period tags every file and leaves the folders; Alt plus Shift plus Period tags duplicate files -- any file with the same content as another in the folder, however named -- which is how a Downloads folder is cleaned.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus I inverts the tags: tagged becomes untagged and untagged tagged, for when it is easier to say what you do not want. Alt plus Comma untags everything except the current item.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A filter and a tag together: Control plus F to show only star dot j p g, Control plus A to tag them all, Shift plus M to move them to Pictures. Three keys, and a year of photographs is sorted.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Shift plus S says the size of the tagged files together, before a copy to a small drive; Alt plus S says the size of the folder you are in.
Key=Shift+S
Hear=3 files, 48 megabytes

[step]

Say=Renaming many: Control plus R takes a pattern with wildcards -- star dot j p e g to star dot j p g -- and renames every tagged file by it, saying the count. F2 renames the current one by name.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Shift plus Z zips the tagged files into one archive named for the folder, or for a name you give; Shift plus U unpacks the current archive into a folder of its name. Both say what they did and how many.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I say the key; the reader says what it does.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus C.
Key=
Hear=Copy
